  <section class="lp-band lp-band--gold">
    <p><strong>“How much will I actually have to pay?”</strong></p>
    <p>The answer depends on your specific Aetna plan.</p>
    <p>Two people can have Aetna insurance and have completely different deductibles, copays, coinsurance, or out-of-pocket costs. That is why understanding your benefits before an appointment can save you an unpleasant surprise later.</p>
    <p>Meaningful Psychiatry is in-network with Aetna for eligible services, but your individual plan determines your actual responsibility.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lp-band">
    <div class="lp-cols lp-cols--middle">
      <div>
        <h2>The three insurance terms you should know</h2>
        <h3>Deductible</h3>
        <p>Your deductible is the amount you may need to pay toward covered healthcare services before your insurance begins sharing costs according to your plan.</p>
        <p>For example, if you have a $1,500 deductible and have not met it yet, you may be responsible for more of the allowed cost of a visit than you would be after meeting the deductible.</p>
        <h3>Copay</h3>
        <p>A copay is a set dollar amount you pay for a covered service.</p>
        <p>Your copay may differ depending on the type of service and your plan.</p>
        <h3>Coinsurance</h3>
        <p>Coinsurance is a percentage of the allowed amount that you are responsible for after applicable deductible requirements are met.</p>
        <p>Your plan might require you to pay a percentage while the insurance company pays the remainder.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="checklist checklist--gold">
        <li>Deductible</li>
        <li>Copay</li>
        <li>Coinsurance</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="lp-band lp-band--gold">
    <h2>Why in-network status matters</h2>
    <p>When a provider is in-network, the provider has a contracted relationship with the insurance plan.</p>
    <p>This generally means the plan’s negotiated rates and in-network cost-sharing rules apply.</p>
    <p>Meaningful Psychiatry is in-network with Aetna for eligible services. However, being in-network does not mean every appointment is free or that every Aetna plan has the same cost.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lp-band">
    <div class="lp-cols lp-cols--middle">
      <div>
        <h2>What should you ask Aetna before your first appointment?</h2>
        <p>Use the number on the back of your insurance card and ask:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Is Meaningful Psychiatry in-network for my specific plan?</li>
          <li>Is the provider I am requesting in-network?</li>
          <li>What is my outpatient mental-health deductible?</li>
          <li>How much of my deductible have I met?</li>
          <li>What is my copay or coinsurance for outpatient mental-health visits?</li>
          <li>Are telehealth visits covered?</li>
          <li>Do I need a referral?</li>
          <li>Do I need prior authorization for any part of my treatment?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>You can also ask our office about the benefit information available to us.</p>
      </div>
      {% include "partials/first-appointment-pill.njk" %}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="lp-band lp-band--gold">
    <h2>What if I have a high deductible?</h2>
    <p>A high-deductible plan can mean you pay more toward covered services before your insurance begins sharing costs according to your plan.</p>
    <p>That does not necessarily mean your insurance is not covering the service. It may mean the service is being applied to your deductible.</p>
    <p>If you are unsure what a bill means, contact your insurance company and ask how the claim was processed.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lp-band">
    <div class="lp-cols lp-cols--middle">
      <div>
        <h2>Aetna coverage is not the same as the cost of your medication</h2>
        <p>There are two different questions:</p>
        <p><strong>How much does my appointment cost?</strong></p>
        <p>and</p>
        <p><strong>How much does my prescription cost?</strong></p>
        <p>Your psychiatric visit is subject to your medical/behavioral-health benefits. Prescription costs are generally determined under your pharmacy benefit.</p>
        <p>Your medication may have a different copay, coinsurance, formulary tier, or prior-authorization requirement.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="checklist checklist--gold">
        <li>Appointment Cost</li>
        <li>Prescription Cost</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="lp-band lp-band--gold">
    <h2>Questions to ask before starting care</h2>
    <p>You do not need to become an insurance expert.</p>
    <p>You simply want enough information to understand what your plan is likely to cover and what you may owe.</p>
    <p>If you are considering psychiatric care with Aetna, start by checking your benefits and confirming the provider’s network status.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="lp-band">
    <h2>For therapists and healthcare providers</h2>
